**Proof.**

We use **Van der Waerden's theorem**: $W(3,3)=27$, i.e., every 3-coloring of $\{0,1,\dots,26\}$ contains a monochromatic 3-term arithmetic progression (3-AP) $a,\ a+d,\ a+2d$ with $d\ge 1$.

**Key observation.** A triangle inscribed in a circle is isosceles iff two of the three arcs between consecutive vertices are equal. If three vertices of a regular $n$-gon sit at indices $a,\ a+d,\ a+2d \pmod n$, the three arcs are $d,\ d,\ n-2d$; hence the triangle is isosceles. Conversely every isosceles triangle with vertices among the $n$-gon's vertices arises this way. Thus *monochromatic isosceles triangles in a regular $n$-gon correspond exactly to monochromatic 3-APs in $\mathbb{Z}_n$.*

Fix a reference regular 27-gon inscribed in the circle, with vertices at angles $2\pi k/27$, $k=0,\dots,26$. For each rotation angle $\theta\in[0,2\pi/27)$, consider the rotated 27-gon with vertices at angles $\theta+2\pi k/27$. Its 27 vertices inherit the given 3-coloring from the circle. By Van der Waerden's theorem, among the indices $\{0,\dots,26\}$ there is a monochromatic 3-AP $(a,a+d,a+2d)$ with $d\ge 1$ and $a+2d\le 26$; the corresponding three vertices
$$\theta+\tfrac{2\pi a}{27},\quad \theta+\tfrac{2\pi(a+d)}{27},\quad \theta+\tfrac{2\pi(a+2d)}{27}$$
form a monochromatic isosceles triangle (arcs $d,d,27-2d$, all positive since $2d\le 26$).

There are only **finitely many** triples $(a,d,c)$ with $a\in\{0,\dots,26\}$, $d\in\{1,\dots,13\}$, and $c$ one of the 3 colors. For each such triple define
$$S_{a,d,c}=\Bigl\{\theta\in[0,2\pi/27):\text{ the three points at angles }\theta+\tfrac{2\pi a}{27},\ \theta+\tfrac{2\pi(a+d)}{27},\ \theta+\tfrac{2\pi(a+2d)}{27}\text{ are all of color }c\Bigr\}.$$

Every $\theta\in[0,2\pi/27)$ belongs to at least one $S_{a,d,c}$, by the previous paragraph. Since $[0,2\pi/27)$ is **uncountable** and is covered by finitely many sets $S_{a,d,c}$, at least one of them — call it $S_{a_0,d_0,c_0}$ — is uncountable (a finite union of countable sets is countable).

For this fixed triple, each $\theta\in S_{a_0,d_0,c_0}$ yields a monochromatic isosceles triangle whose vertex set is a rigid translate (by angle $\theta$) of the fixed 3-point set
$$\Bigl\{\tfrac{2\pi a_0}{27},\ \tfrac{2\pi(a_0+d_0)}{27},\ \tfrac{2\pi(a_0+2d_0)}{27}\Bigr\}.$$

We claim **distinct $\theta$ give distinct triangles**. Two such translates coincide only if the difference of the translation angles is a rotational period of this 3-point set. Every rotational period of a subset of the 27-gon's vertices is a multiple of $2\pi/27$, so the minimal positive period is at least $2\pi/27$. But two distinct $\theta$-values in the half-open interval $[0,2\pi/27)$ differ by less than $2\pi/27$, hence their difference cannot be a nonzero period. Therefore distinct $\theta$ yield distinct triangles.

Since $S_{a_0,d_0,c_0}$ is uncountable, we obtain **uncountably many** distinct monochromatic isosceles triangles inscribed in the circle. In particular, there exist infinitely many. $\blacksquare$

